Exercise 2.14 takes an isomorphism \(f : |D_0| \to |D_1|\) and the one-one neighbourhood correspondence \(\varphi\) of Theorem 2.7 with \(f(\uparrow X) = \uparrow \varphi(X)\), and asks to show that the approximable mapping determined by \(f\) is the relation \(\varphi(X) \subseteq Y\), that consistent neighbourhoods satisfy \(\varphi(X \cap X') = \varphi(X) \cap \varphi(X')\), and that domain isomorphisms correspond exactly to one-one inclusion-preserving correspondences of neighbourhood systems. `phi` selects the neighbourhood whose principal element is the image of the given principal; `phi_mem` puts that neighbourhood in the codomain; `phi_spec` is \(e(\uparrow X) = \uparrow \varphi(X)\); `rel_ofIso_iff` says the relation of `ofIso e` holds between \(X\) and \(Y\) exactly when \(Y\) is a neighbourhood and \(\varphi(X) \subseteq Y\); `phi_inter` is the intersection identity; `example_214` is the conjunction of `phi_inter` and `rel_ofIso_iff`. The intersection argument uses that principals reverse inclusion, that the principal of \(X \cap X'\) is the least upper bound of the two principals, that `phi_spec` rewrites images as principals of the \(\varphi\)-values, that monotonicity of \(e\) puts \(\varphi(X \cap X')\) inside the intersection, and that the inverse isomorphism yields the opposite inclusion. No stem states the converse, that every one-one inclusion-preserving neighbourhood correspondence comes from a domain isomorphism. **Theorem:** partial — the relation and the intersection law are `example_214`, and the exact correspondence of isomorphisms is not a theorem. **Proof:** partial — `phi_inter` and `rel_ofIso_iff` are proved as above, and the remark has no argument.
